A||||3||Ken Wittlief||found cause of raw gas smell - leaking tank filler neck||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||05-31-2001||08:57 AM||||after tanking up a full 9.7 gallons in my '85SE I drove two miles home and crawled around under the car till I found the source of the raw gas smell.<P>  the filler neck was wet, mostly near the top. I dont know if its rusted out or if there is a clamp up there thats loose - cant see anything without taking the plastic wheel well liner out.<P>  also noticed it is getting the sound barrier material in the engine compartment a little wet with gas down in one corner. I am thinking maybe I should break the sound barrier in my Fiero, at least in that area, or would breaking the sound barrier in a Fiero be a bad idea?<P>  anyone else ever have their fillerneck leaky?<P>  Boy these fieros sure are leaky little cars arnt they? so far my gas filler, vacuum lines, the cruise vacumm can, heater core, coolant hoses, oil pan, exhaust manifolds...<P>   I think the only thing on my car that is not leaking is the tranny and the windshield washer fluid bottle.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Alex4mula||05-31-2001||11:21 AM||||I had that problem once about 4-5 years ago. Same place. Only leaked when full due to the neck being on top. First I used a putty sealant but didn't last too long. Took the tank out and took it to a radiator shop where they welded it. The guy cleaned the tank VERY well and let it dry for 1 or 2 days before putting the torch. Not all radiator shops may do that (safety reasons). You may have to shop around. Cost me $30-$40.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||05-31-2001||11:43 AM||||No - mine is not leaking at the tank, its leaking all the way up by the gas cap somewhere, up in the wheel well, almost at the top.<P>  I cant tell from the factory manual drawings how many pieces there are to the whole filler tube, but there must be gaskets and clamps at the gas cap end, and there must be gaskets at the tank.<P>  Ill have too look at it closer with the plastic pulled out of the wheel well - maybe some of that epoxy putty will fix it for now, but I suspect the best fix is to replace the top section of the filler pipe, from the gas cap down a foot or so.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||05-31-2001||03:53 PM||||The filler pipe on my 84 is one piece from the cap all the way down to where it attatches to the tank.  If you are getting a leak from up near the top, it sounds like something is either rusted or cracked.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
